.TH HD-CONFIG 7 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-config \- the hyprdebian configuration database
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-base
keeps the configuration of hyprdebian in
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
the configuration of the system.
Every user may also have a configuration of their own, in
.IR ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json .
Each holds a JSON object.
Each hyprdebian package that has settings keeps them under a key of its
own in that object, usually its package name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
    "hd-example": {
        "enabled": true,
        "port": 8080
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The files are changed with two commands:
.TP
.BR hd-config-update (1)
adds a key, or replaces its value.
.TP
.BR hd-config-remove (1)
removes keys.
.PP
Both change the configuration of the system when root runs them, and the
configuration of the user who runs them otherwise.
.PP
Most settings are of the system, and only the configuration of the system is
read for them.
A program that also reads the configuration of the user says so; it reads
the key there first and, when the configuration of the user does not have
it, in the configuration of the system.
The key
.B hd-theme
of
.BR hd-theme (7)
is read that way.
.PP
Both write the file as a whole to a temporary file next to it and move that
into place, so the file is never seen half written.
They hold a lock on the directory of the file while they change it, so two changes at the same time do not lose each
other's work.
A file is made by the first
.B hd-config-update
that writes it; the package itself installs no configuration.
.SH USING IT FROM A PACKAGE
A package that keeps its settings in the configuration depends on
.B hd-base
and sets its key in its postinst:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-config-update hd-example '{"enabled": true, "port": 8080}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
It removes the key in its postrm on a purge.
By then
.B hd-base
may already be removed, so it checks first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if [ "$1" = "purge" ] && [ \-x /usr/bin/hd-config-remove ]; then
    hd-config-remove hd-example
fi
.fi
.RE
.PP
To read a setting, load the file with any JSON parser, for example with
.BR python3 (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
python3 \-c 'import json; print(json.load(open("/etc/hyprdebian/config.json"))["hd-example"]["port"])'
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The configuration of the system, a JSON object, readable for everyone.
It is not a conffile: removing or purging
.B hd-base
keeps it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json
The configuration of a user, a JSON object.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR hd-config-update (1),
.BR hd-config-remove (1),
.BR hd-theme (7)
